<title>SOUTH AFRICA: Pick 'n Pay yr shr jumps, div surges.</title>
<headline>Pick 'n Pay yr shr jumps, div surges.</headline>
<dateline>CAPE TOWN 1997-04-09</dateline>
<text>
<p>Year ended Feb 28, 1997</p>
<p>			(million rand unless stated)</p>
<p>    Shr, attrib (cts)	28.65	vs    22.55</p>
<p>    Shr, headline (cts)    29.33	vs    22.55</p>
<p>    Div (cts)		  22.80	vs    13.50</p>
<p>    Attrib income	   134.5	 vs   105.9</p>
<p>    Exceptional cost	  3.2	 vs     -</p>
<p>    Pre-tax		   221.9	 vs   177.3</p>
<p>    Tax			  73.5	 vs    56.3</p>
<p>    Trading income	  159.6	 vs   141.4</p>
<p>    Interest received	68.2	 vs    41.2</p>
<p>    Investment income	15.1	 vs    11.4</p>
<p>    Interest paid	    17.8	 vs    16.7</p>
<p>    Operating profit	225.1	 vs   177.3</p>
<p>Outside shareholders'</p>
<p>     interest		  13.9	 vs    15.1</p>
<p>    Turnover		9,793.5	 vs 9,169.4</p>
<p>Div payable on date to be advised</p>
<p>to shareholders on register May 2</p>
<p>Full name: Pick 'n Pay Stores Ltd</p>
<p>NOTE - The supermarket chain said it would offer capitalisation shares in lieu of the final cash dividend of 14.00 cents and a bonus 30th birthday dividend of 5.00 cents.</p>
<p>Shareholders in Pick 'n Pay Holdings Ltd (Pikwik) will be offered capaitalisation shares or a final cash dividend of 6.91 cents plus a bonus 2.47 cents. Pikwik's only asset is its 51.9 percent investment in Pick 'n Pay Stores.  </p>
<p>Analysts polled by Reuters had forecast eps of 28.2 with a range 27.4-29.3 and a dividend of 16.6 with a range 15.7-17.2.</p>
<p>-- Cape Town newsroom +27 21 252 238</p>
</text>
